EXT. MANGROVE SWAMP - DUSK

Brackish water, knotted roots rising like fingers. A small aluminum boat lies keel-up in the shallows, half sunk in silt. Mosquitoes hang in veils.

RUTH ABERNETHY-SOLIS (50s, torn khakis, a field notebook held above the waterline) wades thigh-deep toward the wreck. Behind her, her nephew KIERAN (20s, soaked, a coil of rope over his shoulder) hauls himself over a root. A few paces further, a guide named TOMASI (60s, bare feet, a machete resting loosely at his side) watches the treeline.

Ruth reaches the boat. Runs her palm along the hull. Wipes the silt from a plate near the stern. A stenciled name, scratched but legible: the same one as in her notebook.

She holds the notebook open beside it. Compares. Closes it without a word.

Kieran lowers the rope. Tomasi turns his head. Far off, something large moves through the water, rippling the surface in a long slow V.

None of them speaks. Ruth tucks the notebook inside her shirt. Kieran steps closer to her. Tomasi lifts the machete, a few inches, and holds it there.

The ripple vanishes. The mosquitoes whine. Ruth nods once, and the three of them begin to wade, very carefully, back the way they came.
